{
  peppy_schema: "contract/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "base_navigation",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "Moves of a robot's mobile base that keep clear of what the robot's sensors see: a navigation to a pose around the obstacles, a turn in place (spin), a drive ahead (drive_on_heading) and a drive back (back_up).",
      "In a navigation, the implementer plans a path around the obstacles its sensors have seen, follows it, plans again as it sees more, slows and stops before an obstacle ahead and before a drop in the floor, and comes to rest at the goal. navigate_to_pose, spin, drive_on_heading and back_up carry the names of Nav2's actions. base_motion's move_base is the move of the base that checks no obstacle. Every fact about the world comes from the robot's own sensors. The implementer leads the base over the drive_link pairing, and reads the base's linear_limits, angular_limits and footprint from base_state's describe_base. A navigation whose target is in the map frame uses the implementer's loaded map; an implementer that reads no map has no map loaded.",
      "The robot frame and the odometry frame are base_state's, base_footprint and odom in transforms, and the map frame, map in transforms, is the frame of the loaded map. Each navigation names its frame and its time: its target is a pose_stamped in a frame as transforms names frames, or with no frame in the robot frame, at a timestamp on the clock of transforms, or with no timestamp for the latest time. When it accepts a navigation, the implementer places it by its tree at its timestamp, as transforms defines a lookup. A command with no timestamp is placed by the latest transforms when the implementer accepts it; a target in the robot frame with no timestamp names where the robot frame stands then, fixed to the floor, and one with the timestamp of a report names where the robot frame stood then. A navigation it cannot place is refused with the failure kind of the lookup, its message naming the cause as transform_lookup's does. A navigation whose target is in the map frame runs in the map frame, on the loaded map; one whose target is in any other frame is placed in the odometry frame and runs in it, as Nav2's navigator places a goal in the frame it navigates in. A spin, a drive_on_heading and a back_up hold no pose: each turns or drives from where the robot frame stands when it is accepted, and runs in the odometry frame, as Nav2's behaviors run in their local frame. A navigation's feedback and every result give the robot frame's pose in the frame the goal runs in, measured at their timestamp. A target whose position, placed in the frame it runs in, has a z farther than 1e-9 m from 0, or whose orientation, placed in the frame it runs in, turns by more than 1e-9 rad about an axis other than +z, is refused with `rejected`, the message naming the part, its value placed in the frame the navigation runs in, and what to send: a target in that frame or the robot frame with a position [x, y, 0] and an orientation [0, 0, sin(yaw/2), cos(yaw/2)]. An orientation whose squared norm lies 0.01 or more from 1 is refused with `rejected`, the tolerance of tf2's buffer that transforms names; the implementer normalizes each orientation it takes. A navigation's target gives an orientation, as Nav2's NavigateToPose takes a whole pose. The durations and timeouts a goal names are on the clock the implementer's instance reads, the simulation clock in a simulation.",
      "The implementer keeps a grid of the floor from its sensors, in the odometry frame: a square with the implementer's grid side. The grid centers on the base, keeping what the square still holds, when the implementer accepts a goal and with each reading while the goal runs, as Nav2's local costmap rolls with the robot. It also centers on the base with each reading while no goal runs and the implementer streams a leader's setpoints of drive_link to the base. A goal that runs in the odometry frame has its end inside the grid centered on the base when the implementer accepts it, as the checks of a goal state: a navigation ends at its goal, a spin where the base stands, and a drive_on_heading or a back_up its distance ahead of or behind where the base stands. The grid reads one or more sensors, each a depth camera of rgbd_camera fixed to the base, its carried_by in sensor_mounts empty; it reads no sensor that a part carries, and the node's description names the sensors it reads and which of them see the floor. A reading is one message of a sensor the grid reads, a depth frame of rgbd_camera. A sensor is silent while no reading of it went into the grid within the implementer's source timeout; a reading the implementer cannot place goes into none. An obstacle is what the sensors see between the implementer's two obstacle heights, the lower above the floor, or a drop in the floor; the upper height is at least the robot's height with each part that raises it, such as a lift, at its highest. A sensor's return marks an obstacle, and its ray counts toward a drop, only while the return lies within the implementer's obstacle range of the sensor in the floor's plane, as Nav2's obstacle layer marks only the points of a source within its obstacle_max_range. A cell that a sensor sees an obstacle in is occupied, and it is free again after the sensors see through it, by the implementer's sensor model; a cell never seen counts as free. A drop is a place where the rays of one reading of a sensor that sees the floor cross the floor's plane and read the floor deeper than the implementer's step; its cell stays occupied until the padded footprint clears it or the grid is emptied. The padded footprint is the footprint of describe_base grown by the implementer's padding. With each reading, the grid drops every return that lies within the base's unpadded outline placed where the base stood at the return's time, at every height, so what the sensors see of the robot itself, such as its base or a parked arm, marks nothing; and it clears the cells that the padded footprint covers where the base stood at the reading's time, as Nav2's obstacle layer clears the footprint, so a cell the base stood on stays free until a sensor sees an obstacle in it again. The grid empties when the odometry starts over. A map-frame navigation also counts occupied each cell that the loaded map holds occupied, under the footprint too. A pose is in collision when an occupied cell lies within the radius of the largest circle about the robot frame's origin inside the padded footprint, so the test holds at any heading. The padded footprint meets an occupied cell at a pose when an occupied cell lies under its outline there, or a corner of the outline lies outside the grid, as Nav2's footprint cost reads a pose; the sweeps and the slowing's grid check read every pose so, and the controller's arc reads so each pose whose robot frame origin lies on the grid and passes a pose whose origin lies outside it, as Nav2's regulated pure pursuit does. A moving base keeps the whole padded footprint off every occupied cell, and keeps clear no part outside the footprint, such as an outstretched arm: a goal of this contract moves the base only while drive_states reports parts_within_footprint true, and what a part holds is the caller's to know.",
      "No goal of this contract moves the base sideways, so an omnidirectional base drives as a differential one: a navigation moves the base forward and turns it, and moves it backward only in the back-up of its recoveries; a spin turns it in place, a drive_on_heading drives it forward and a back_up backward. Every goal of this contract needs measured odometry, which describe_base names in odometry_source. Every goal slows the base so that, at the velocity it commands, the padded footprint meets no point and no cell that the slowing checks, at any of the implementer's simulation steps within the larger of the implementer's time before a collision and the sum of its source timeout and the longer of the times that describe_base's linear_limits max_deceleration along x and its angular_limits max_deceleration take to bring the base to rest from the fastest velocity of their axis; the slowing brings the base to rest before the footprint meets one. At each such step, the slowing checks the padded footprint in two ways. It checks each sensor's newest points: the points of the obstacles and the drops that the sensor's newest reading sees, whether or not the grid holds them, less those within describe_base's footprint, the robot itself. The padded footprint meets each such point that lies inside it, as Nav2's collision monitor checks a source's points against its polygon. It also checks the grid along the padded footprint's outline: the padded footprint meets each occupied cell that its outline crosses, and each cell off the grid, as Nav2's controller checks a pose by the cost of its footprint along the outline's cells and counts a cell off the costmap lethal. A navigation plans a path when it starts. It plans again when a try starts, and at the implementer's planning rate while it follows, except while the path it has, from that path's pose nearest the robot frame's origin to the goal, is shorter than the implementer's near-goal distance and has no pose in collision or outside the grid; it then keeps that path. A plan that finds no path leaves the navigation no path to keep, as Nav2's ComputePathToPose gives an empty path when it fails. The navigation reaches its goal once the robot frame's origin stands within position_tolerance_m of the goal, with the path left to it within the implementer's path length tolerance, and then its heading within orientation_tolerance_rad of the target's heading; an orientation tolerance of pi or more leaves the heading free. The navigation completes once it reaches its goal, as Nav2's goal checker completes a goal by its pose and the path left; its result gives where the robot frame stood then.",
      "navigate_to_pose, spin, drive_on_heading and back_up have the mark `moves_robot`, and clear_grid has none: each MCP server, `peppy node call` and each lease holder asks the daemon (`robot_leases.check_move`) whether it may move the robot before it calls one of the four.",
      "One goal of this contract runs at a time, whichever of its four actions it is. A goal that arrives while another runs replaces it: the running goal ends cancelled, with a reason that says a new goal replaced it, and its result gives where the robot frame stood when the new goal was accepted; the base moves on into the new goal at its first setpoint. A goal that is refused leaves the running goal as it was. A goal counts as before the base moves until it streams its first setpoint, also while the base still moves after the goal before it. While a goal runs, its action gives feedback once per step of the implementer's controller and at least once a second.",
      "drive_link decides whether the base follows the implementer, and the implementer learns it from drive_states. A drive_states that reports a follow_state other than following ends the goal in flight at once, by that follow_state. With robot_stop or follower_goal, the goal ends cancelled, with a reason that names that state, and its result gives where the robot frame stood when drive_states first reported it. With base_mode, it fails with `attempt_failed`, the message naming the mode drive_states reports. With base_silence, it fails with `resource_unavailable`, the message saying that a silence of the base's motion sensing holds the base. While the newest drive_states reports a follow_state other than following, the implementer streams no setpoint. So robot_stop's stop ends a goal of this contract cancelled, whoever started it, when drive_states reports robot_stop before it reports base_mode or base_silence, and before more than 0.5 s pass with no drive_states. While drive_states reports following and parts_within_footprint false, the goal in flight fails with `attempt_failed`, the message saying that a limb left the footprint.",
      "The implementer reads base_state's describe_base each time drive_states reports a new odometry_start_timestamp, the first drive_states included. A goal is checked in this order. A goal that arrives before drive_states first arrives, or before the read for its newest odometry_start_timestamp answers, waits for that answer, up to the implementer's source timeout; it is refused with `resource_unavailable` when the read fails or the timeout passes first, and while the answer describes a base the implementer cannot drive, such as one whose linear_limits max_velocity along x is 0, the message naming why; and with `precondition_failed` while the answer names commanded odometry. It is refused with `rejected` for a value it does not take: a target whose orientation is no unit quaternion, as the contract's description says; a navigation whose target is in the map frame and is not planar, as the contract's description says, or lies outside the loaded map while a map is loaded; a max_speed_m_s below 0, above the larger magnitude of describe_base's linear_limits min_velocity and max_velocity along x, or above 0 and below its linear_limits deadband along x; a spin whose angle_rad is above 0 while describe_base's angular_limits max_rate is 0, or below 0 while its min_rate is 0; a drive_on_heading or a back_up whose end lies outside the grid centered on the base, whose distance_m is not above 0, or whose speed_m_s is not above 0, lies below describe_base's linear_limits deadband along x, or lies above the magnitude of its linear_limits limit along x in the drive's direction, max_velocity for a drive_on_heading and min_velocity for a back_up; a timeout_s below 0; a tolerance that is not positive; and a number that is not finite. A navigation whose target is in the map frame is refused with `not_localized` while no map is loaded or the base is not localized in it. A navigation whose target is in any other frame is placed in the odometry frame, and refused with the failure kind of the lookup when the implementer cannot place it; then with `rejected` when its target, placed, is not planar, as the contract's description says, or lies outside the grid centered on the base. A goal is refused with `resource_unavailable` while more than 0.5 s have passed since the newest drive_states arrived, while the newest drive_states reports follow_state base_silence, and while one or more sensors the grid reads are silent; the message names what it waits for, each silent sensor by name. Then it is refused with `precondition_failed` while the newest drive_states reports follow_state base_mode, the message naming the mode and, while that mode is neither standing nor moving, what brings the base to standing; with `precondition_failed` while the newest drive_states reports parts_within_footprint false, the message naming postures' move_to_home; with `leader_streaming` while a leader that the implementer itself follows on drive_link streams, as drive_link defines it; and with `precondition_failed` while follow_state is robot_stop or follower_goal. The message of a refusal names its cause and what to do: a goal refused for follow_state robot_stop or base_silence, or for base_mode while that mode is standing or moving, says to send it again more than 0.6 s after the refusal; a goal refused with `not_localized` says that no map is loaded, and to send the target in odom or the robot frame; a goal refused with `rejected` for a target that is not planar names the part and what to send, as the contract's description says.",
      "Before the base moves, a navigation fails with `start_in_collision` when the base is in collision, and otherwise with `unreachable` when its goal is. A navigation runs in tries, each of which plans a path, or keeps the one it has as this contract keeps a path, and follows it. A plan that finds no path empties the grid, as clear_grid empties it, and plans once more on the readings that arrive after the clearing; each plan has one such retry. A follow fails when the base makes no progress, and when the controller gives no command that keeps the padded footprint off every occupied cell along its arc within the implementer's arc look-ahead time, for longer than the implementer's failure tolerance since the start of the follow or the newest command that did. The implementer commands zero velocity at each step without such a command. A failed follow empties the grid the same way and follows the path once more on the readings that arrive after the clearing, and the following of a try has one such retry. A try fails when its plan fails after the retry, when its plan finds the base or the goal in collision, and when its following fails after the retry; a follow that ends because a plan failed has not failed. When a try fails, the navigation runs its next recovery when the newest follow of the navigation that ended had failed, or the newest plan found no path. Otherwise the navigation fails: with `start_in_collision` when the newest plan found the base in collision, with `unreachable` when it found the goal in collision, and with `attempt_failed` for another cause. The recoveries run in this order, each at most once in a navigation: a clearing of the grid, as clear_grid clears it; a turn in place counterclockwise by the implementer's recovery angle; a wait for the implementer's wait time; and a back-up by the implementer's back-up distance at its back-up speed, at most the navigation's max_speed_m_s when that is above 0. Where that bound lies below the slowest speed of a straight drive, the back-up commands the bound. A navigation that replaces another goal starts its recoveries afresh. After the clearing, the turn or the wait, the navigation starts a new try, and a turn that fails passes straight to the wait. Once the back-up ends, whether it covered its distance or failed, the navigation fails: with `blocked` when the newest follow of the navigation that ended had failed, and with `no_path` otherwise. The wait streams zero velocity for its time. A base makes no progress while its origin moves less than the implementer's progress distance within its progress time. A map-frame navigation whose base stops being localized, or whose loaded map changes, fails with `not_localized`, the message naming the cause.",
      "A turn is the turn of a navigation's recoveries or a spin, and a straight drive is the back-up of a navigation's recoveries, a drive_on_heading or a back_up. Each runs within its timeout: for a recovery the implementer's, and for a goal its timeout_s, where 0 sets none. A turn commands, at each step of the controller, the rate from which describe_base's angular_limits max_deceleration brings the base to rest at the end of the turn, at least the larger of the implementer's slowest turn rate and describe_base's angular_limits deadband and at most the magnitude of describe_base's angular_limits limit in the turn's direction, max_rate counterclockwise and min_rate clockwise. It turns its whole angle once the odometry measures it, and fails when its timeout passes first, and when its sweep meets an occupied cell: the padded footprint turned from where the base stands at that rate by each step of the controller, up to the implementer's simulate-ahead time and to less than the turn made so far, so the first step of a turn checks no pose. A straight drive commands, at each step of the controller, its speed along the robot frame's +X for a drive_on_heading and along -X otherwise, or, when that speed lies further from the command of the step before (0 before the first step) than describe_base's linear_limits max_acceleration along x times the time between the controller's steps for a growing speed, or its max_deceleration along x times that time for a falling one, the velocity that far from that command toward that speed, as Nav2's drive_on_heading ramps its speed; it commands less where describe_base's linear_limits max_deceleration along x must bring the base to rest at its distance, at least the larger of the implementer's slowest speed for that direction and describe_base's linear_limits deadband along x and at most the magnitude of describe_base's linear_limits limit along x in that direction, max_velocity forward and min_velocity backward. On a base whose linear_limits min_velocity along x is 0, the back-up of a recovery fails at once. A straight drive covers its distance once the robot frame's origin stands that far from where the drive started, and fails when its timeout passes first, and when its sweep meets an occupied cell: the padded footprint placed where the base stands and then along its heading in its direction at the commanded speed by each step of the controller, up to the simulate-ahead time and to less than the distance left.",
      "Once the odometry measures the whole turn of a spin, or the whole distance of a drive_on_heading or a back_up, the goal completes, as Nav2's spin, drive_on_heading and back_up complete; its result gives where the robot frame stood then. It fails with `blocked` when its sweep meets an occupied cell, and with `timeout` when its timeout passes first. A goal of this contract fails with `resource_unavailable` when a sensor the grid reads falls silent, when the odometry starts over, and when more than 0.5 s pass with no drive_states. A goal fails with `attempt_failed` when it cannot go on for another reason. Each completion, each cancel and each failure ends its goal at once. A completed or a cancelled goal gives its result: where the robot frame stood at the timestamp of the newest drive_states when the goal ended, or, when a follow_state other than following ended it, at the timestamp of the drive_states that first reported that follow_state. A cancelled goal also gives the reason of the cancel, and a failed goal gives no result. When one step of the implementer's controller gives a goal more than one ending, the goal takes the first of them in this order: a cancel; the acceptance of a new goal; a follow_state other than following; a new odometry_start_timestamp; more than 0.5 s with no drive_states; a silent sensor; parts_within_footprint false; the passing of its timeout_s; a map-frame navigation's `not_localized`; its completion; every other failure. When a goal ends while at most 0.5 s have passed since the implementer's newest setpoint and the newest drive_states reports following, the implementer streams zero velocity for its release burst, 0.25 s and at least 3 setpoints, and then streams nothing. So one lost setpoint cannot lose the stop. A drive_states that reports a follow_state other than following ends the burst, and so does the first setpoint of the next goal. A goal that ends during a burst starts the burst again: 0.25 s and at least 3 setpoints from that ending. The burst's zero setpoints slow the base through base_state's limiter, and drive_link's follower brings the base to rest when the stream ends. So after a goal completes, the base can come to rest beyond the goal's end by the distance and the turn it covers while it slows.",
    ],
  },
  failure_kinds: {
    unreachable: "The goal is in collision with an obstacle the sensors have seen or the map holds",
    start_in_collision: "The base stands in collision with an obstacle the sensors have seen or the map holds",
    no_path: "No path joins the base to the goal around the obstacles, after the recoveries",
    blocked: "The moving base found no way forward: a navigation after its recoveries, or a spin, a drive_on_heading or a back_up whose sweep meets an obstacle",
    not_localized: "The map-frame goal has no loaded map, or the base is not localized in it",
    leader_streaming: "Another leader of drive_link drives the base",
    unknown_frame: "A frame of the request is named by no transform the implementer has received that passed the checks of transforms",
    no_chain: "A frame of the request and the frame the implementer places it in are in trees that share no frame",
    extrapolation: "A moving transform on the chain has no value at the request's time: the implementer holds no stamp of it, or the time is before the oldest stamp it holds of it or after its newest",
    broken_tree: "A frame whose transform to its parent the chain needs has transforms from two publishers, or the chain runs through a cycle",
  },
  interfaces: {
    actions: [
      {
        name: "navigate_to_pose",
        description: "Drive the robot frame to a pose and bring the base to rest there, around the obstacles the sensors see. Feedback gives where the robot frame stands in the frame the navigation runs in, the length of the path left, an estimate of the time left and the recoveries run. The result gives where the robot frame stood in that frame when the navigation ended, how far the base drove and how long the action took.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            target: {
              $type: "object",
              $description: "The pose to drive the robot frame to: planar, as the contract's description says, with an orientation; in the map frame it lies inside the loaded map, and in any other frame, placed in the odometry frame, inside the grid centered on the base.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames; absent, the robot frame." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $optional: true, $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold. A command leaves it out for the latest time; a report leaves it out for values that hold at every time." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
            position_tolerance_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "The navigation reaches the goal once the robot frame's origin stands within this distance (m) of it and the path left to it is no longer than the implementer's path length tolerance." },
            orientation_tolerance_rad: { $type: "f64", $description: "The navigation reaches the goal once the robot frame's heading then stands within this angle (rad) of the target's heading; pi or more leaves the heading free." },
            max_speed_m_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "The fastest the base may move (m/s), as the norm of its linear_velocity, 0 or above; 0 = describe_base's limits alone." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            current_pose: {
              $type: "object",
              $description: "Where the robot frame stands in the frame the navigation runs in, measured at timestamp.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
            distance_remaining_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "Length of the planned path from the robot frame's origin to the goal (m); while no path is planned, the straight-line distance." },
            angle_remaining_rad: { $type: "f64", $description: "Magnitude of the shorter angle from the robot frame's heading to the target's heading (rad, in [0, pi])." },
            estimated_time_remaining_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "An estimate of the time left to reach the goal (s): distance_remaining_m over the base's measured linear speed, the norm of drive_states' linear_velocity; 0 while that speed is at or below 0.01 m/s or distance_remaining_m is at or below 0.1 m." },
            recoveries: { $type: "u32", $description: "How many clearings, turns, waits and back-ups the navigation has started since it was accepted, the clearing of each retry included." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            final_pose: {
              $type: "object",
              $description: "Where the robot frame stood in the frame the navigation ran in when the navigation ended, measured at timestamp: at the newest drive_states then, or, when a follow_state other than following ended the navigation, at the drive_states that first reported that follow_state.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
            distance_driven_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "Length of the way the base drove, by its odometry (m)." },
            action_time: { $type: "f64", $description: "Wall-clock duration of the action (s)." },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["unreachable", "start_in_collision", "no_path", "blocked", "not_localized", "leader_streaming", "unknown_frame", "no_chain", "extrapolation", "broken_tree"],
        marks: ["moves_robot"],
      },
      {
        name: "spin",
        description: "Turn the base in place by angle_rad, as this contract runs a turn, and bring it to rest. Feedback gives the angle turned. The result gives where the robot frame stood in the odometry frame when the spin ended and how long the action took.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            angle_rad: { $type: "f64", $description: "The turn (rad), counterclockwise positive and clockwise negative, any finite angle; beyond a full turn the base turns more than once." },
            timeout_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "How long (s) from its acceptance the goal has to turn its whole angle before it fails with `timeout`, 0 or above; 0 = no bound." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            angle_turned_rad: { $type: "f64", $description: "The angle the robot frame has turned since the goal was accepted, by the odometry (rad), counterclockwise positive, summed over the turn, so its magnitude can exceed pi." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            final_pose: {
              $type: "object",
              $description: "Where the robot frame stood in the odometry frame when the spin ended, measured at timestamp: at the newest drive_states then, or, when a follow_state other than following ended the spin, at the drive_states that first reported that follow_state.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
            action_time: { $type: "f64", $description: "Wall-clock duration of the action (s)." },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["blocked", "leader_streaming"],
        marks: ["moves_robot"],
      },
      {
        name: "drive_on_heading",
        description: "Drive the base forward along +X by distance_m at speed_m_s, as this contract runs a straight drive, and bring it to rest. Feedback gives the distance traveled. The result gives where the robot frame stood in the odometry frame when the drive ended and how long the action took.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            distance_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "How far (m) the robot frame's origin drives forward, above 0; its end lies inside the grid centered on the base." },
            speed_m_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "The speed to drive at (m/s), above 0, at least describe_base's linear_limits deadband along x and at most its max_velocity along x." },
            timeout_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "How long (s) from its acceptance the goal has to cover its distance before it fails with `timeout`, 0 or above; 0 = no bound." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            distance_traveled_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "The distance of the robot frame's origin from where it stood when the goal was accepted, by the odometry (m)." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            final_pose: {
              $type: "object",
              $description: "Where the robot frame stood in the odometry frame when the drive ended, measured at timestamp: at the newest drive_states then, or, when a follow_state other than following ended the drive, at the drive_states that first reported that follow_state.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
            action_time: { $type: "f64", $description: "Wall-clock duration of the action (s)." },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["blocked", "leader_streaming"],
        marks: ["moves_robot"],
      },
      {
        name: "back_up",
        description: "Drive the base backward along -X by distance_m at speed_m_s, as this contract runs a straight drive, and bring it to rest. Feedback gives the distance traveled. The result gives where the robot frame stood in the odometry frame when the back-up ended and how long the action took.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            distance_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "How far (m) the robot frame's origin drives backward, above 0; its end lies inside the grid centered on the base." },
            speed_m_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "The speed to drive at (m/s), above 0, at least describe_base's linear_limits deadband along x and at most the magnitude of its min_velocity along x." },
            timeout_s: { $type: "f64", $description: "How long (s) from its acceptance the goal has to cover its distance before it fails with `timeout`, 0 or above; 0 = no bound." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            distance_traveled_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "The distance of the robot frame's origin from where it stood when the goal was accepted, by the odometry (m)." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            final_pose: {
              $type: "object",
              $description: "Where the robot frame stood in the odometry frame when the back-up ended, measured at timestamp: at the newest drive_states then, or, when a follow_state other than following ended the back-up, at the drive_states that first reported that follow_state.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
            action_time: { $type: "f64", $description: "Wall-clock duration of the action (s)." },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["blocked", "leader_streaming"],
        marks: ["moves_robot"],
      },
    ],
    services: [
      {
        name: "clear_grid",
        description: "Empty the grid of the floor: every cell counts as never seen, and a navigation in flight plans on what the sensors see from then on. The cells the loaded map holds occupied stay occupied for a map-frame navigation. It gives an error reply, with `attempt_failed`, only when the clearing itself cannot be carried out.",
      },
    ],
  },
}
